C# Replace() Method

2.Replace(oldValue,newValue)

The Replace() method is used to replace all occurrences of a specific word or character in a string with a new word or character.

Syntax-
-------
string newString = originalString.Replace(oldValue, newValue);

oldValue: the text you want to change

newValue: the text you want to put in its place

Simple Example-
-------------
string sentence = "I love Java";
string updated = sentence.Replace("Java", "C#");
Console.WriteLine(updated); // Output: I love C#

Character Replacement Example-

string data = "a-b-c-d";
string clean = data.Replace("-", ",");
Console.WriteLine(clean);  // Output: a,b,c,d

Replace Letters Inside Words-

string title = "CSharp Programming";
string updatedTitle = title.Replace("a", "@");
Console.WriteLine(updatedTitle); // Output: CSh@rp Progr@mming

Case Sensitivity-
----------------
The Replace() method is case-sensitive:

string name = "Venkatesh";
Console.WriteLine(name.Replace("v", "X")); // Output: Venkatesh (no change)

"v" ≠ "V" — so nothing is replaced.



Real-Life Use Cases-


| Use Case                | Example                                   |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Replacing tech names    | `"I love Java"` → `"I love C#"`           |
| Masking sensitive words | `"This is bad"` → `"This is ***"`         |
| Cleaning user input     | Replace `-` with space, or `@` with blank |
| Updating templates      | `"Hello {name}"` → `"Hello Venkatesh"`    |
| Formatting data         | `"01-01-2024"` → `"01/01/2024"`           |


Summary Table-
-------------
| Method Call             | What It Does                   |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `Replace("old", "new")` | Replaces text                  |
| `Replace("a", "@")`     | Replaces every `a` with `@`    |
| Case sensitive          | `"a"` ≠ `"A"`                  |
| Returns a new string    | Original string doesn't change |


